    "evidence_text": "Robustly Leveraging Prior Knowledge in Text Classification\tPrior knowledge has been shown very useful to address many natural language processing tasks.\n\nMany approaches have been proposed to formalise a variety of knowledge, however, whether the proposed approach is robust or sensitive to the knowledge supplied to the model has rarely been discussed.\n\nA key problem arisen here, is how to leverage such knowledge to guide the learning process, an interesting problem for both NLP and machine learning communities.\n\nFirst, to leverage prior knowledge to label data  ,  .\n\nthe balance among classes.\n\nRelated Work\tThere have been much work that incorporate prior knowledge into learning, and two related lines are surveyed here.\n\nThe other is to constrain the model directly with prior knowledge.\n\nAndrzejewski et al.andrzejewski2011framework proposed a framework in which general domain knowledge can be easily incorporated into LDA.\n\nThere are also some active learning methods trying to use prior knowledge.\n\nConclusion and Discussions\tThis paper investigates into the problem of how to leverage prior knowledge robustly in learning models.\n\nTo the best of our knowledge, this is the first work to address the robustness problem of leveraging knowledge, and may inspire other research.\n\nThe KL\n\nThe fact suggests that additional knowledge does benefit the model.",
